# Svelte 5 Runes

Cursor: Svelte 5 (October 2024) introduced runes, a compile-time language extension that replaces the implicit `let` reactivity, `$:` reactive statements, `export let` props, and stores-for-everything pattern of Svelte 4. Target: `svelte ^5.55.7`.

The companion `sveltekit-anti-patterns` rule rejects the pre-runes shapes (entries #1, #2, #3, #11, #12, #13, #14, #15). This rule explains the positive form.

## `$state` for reactive locals

`$state` returns a reactive proxy. Mutate in place; consumers re-read.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
  let user = $state({ name: 'Ada', age: 36 })
  let items = $state<string[]>([])

  function inc() { count++ }
  function rename() { user.name = 'Grace' }
  function add(s: string) { items.push(s) }
</script>
```

Deep state: nested objects and arrays are recursively proxied. Reading a nested field is tracked; writing is reactive. Reads through a destructured local are NOT tracked once the local is captured.

## `$state.raw` for large or immutable data

Opts out of deep proxying. Mutate by replacement, never in place.

```ts
let rows = $state.raw<Row[]>([])
function add(r: Row) { rows = [...rows, r] }    // GOOD: replace
// rows.push(r)                                  // BAD: mutation is invisible
```

Use when:

- Arrays larger than a few hundred elements where proxy overhead matters.
- Class instances you do not want auto-proxied.
- Data you serialise to JSON; the proxy adds enumerable property weight.

## `$state.snapshot` for serialisation

Returns a plain (non-proxy) deep clone of a reactive value. Use before sending state to an API, `JSON.stringify`, or `structuredClone`.

```ts
const cart = $state({ items: [] as Item[] })
const payload = $state.snapshot(cart)  // plain object
await fetch('/api/cart', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(payload) })
```

## `$derived` for pure derivations

Recomputes lazily when any read state changes. Do NOT use `$effect` to write a derived value (anti-pattern #11).

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
  let doubled = $derived(count * 2)
  let label = $derived(`${count} clicks`)
</script>
```

For multi-line derivations, use `$derived.by(() => { ... })`:

```ts
let sorted = $derived.by(() => {
  const copy = [...items]
  copy.sort((a, b) => a.priority - b.priority)
  return copy
})
```

## `$effect` for browser-only side effects

Runs after the DOM updates. Tracks every `$state` read synchronously before the first `await`. Returns a cleanup callback. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$effect

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  let id = $state(1)

  $effect(() => {
    const ctrl = new AbortController()
    fetch(`/api/${id}`, { signal: ctrl.signal }).then(/* ... */)
    return () => ctrl.abort()
  })
</script>
```

Effects only run in the browser. They never run during SSR. If you need a value in the SSR HTML, compute it in `+page.ts` / `+page.server.ts` `load` and pass it as a prop.

`$effect.pre` runs BEFORE DOM update; useful for measurement before commit.

`$effect.tracking()` returns whether the calling code is inside a tracking context.

`$effect.root()` creates a manual root for effects outside a component lifecycle (rare; for libraries).

## `$props` for typed component inputs

Replaces `export let`. Defaults via destructure. Type via inline annotation.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import type { Snippet } from 'svelte'

  type Props = {
    title: string
    count?: number
    children?: Snippet
    onsave?: (value: string) => void
  }

  let { title, count = 0, children, onsave }: Props = $props()
</script>
```

`...rest` props:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  let { title, ...rest }: { title: string; [k: string]: unknown } = $props()
</script>
<button {...rest}>{title}</button>
```

## `$props.id()` for SSR-safe instance IDs (Svelte 5.20+)

Returns a unique, stable, SSR-safe ID for the current component instance. Use for `aria-labelledby`, `aria-describedby`, `for`. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$props#$props.id

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  const uid = $props.id()
</script>
<label for="{uid}-email">Email</label>
<input id="{uid}-email" type="email" />
```

## `$bindable` for opt-in two-way binding

Props are read-only by default. `$bindable()` marks a prop as writable so the parent can `bind:` to it.

```svelte
<!-- child: TextInput.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  let { value = $bindable('') }: { value?: string } = $props()
</script>
<input bind:value />
```

```svelte
<!-- parent -->
<script lang="ts">
  let text = $state('')
</script>
<TextInput bind:value={text} />
```

## `$host` for custom-element host element (rare)

When a component is compiled as a custom element, `$host()` returns the host `HTMLElement`. Use only inside `<svelte:options customElement="..." />` components.

```svelte
<svelte:options customElement="my-counter" />
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
  function emit() {
    $host().dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('inc', { detail: count }))
  }
</script>
```

## `$inspect` for debug logging

Logs the value (and any change) to the console in dev. No-op in production.

```ts
let count = $state(0)
$inspect(count)  // logs every change

// Add a label via the custom-logger callback (the second arg is not a label):
$inspect(cart).with((type, val) => console.warn('cart', type, val))
```

## Why these matter for LLM output

Pre-October-2024 trained models will produce:

- `let count = 0` for component state (anti-pattern #1)
- `$: doubled = count * 2` (anti-pattern #2)
- `export let title: string` (anti-pattern #3)
- `$effect(() => { doubled = count * 2 })` instead of `$derived` (anti-pattern #11)
- `$state` for huge arrays without `$state.raw` (anti-pattern #13)
- `$effect` reads after `await` expecting tracking (anti-pattern #15)

Each is the documented Svelte 4 shape. Each is wrong in Svelte 5 runes mode. The rune form is the documented shape on `svelte.dev` since the 5.0 release.

## Quick-reference combination

A small Svelte 5 component using the rune trio together:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import type { Snippet } from 'svelte'

  type Props = {
    initial?: number
    children?: Snippet<[count: number]>
    onchange?: (n: number) => void
  }

  let { initial = 0, children, onchange }: Props = $props()

  let count = $state(initial)
  let doubled = $derived(count * 2)
  const uid = $props.id()

  $effect(() => { onchange?.(count) })
</script>

<div>
  <label for="{uid}-c">Count</label>
  <input id="{uid}-c" type="number" bind:value={count} />
  <p>Doubled: {doubled}</p>
  {@render children?.(count)}
</div>
```
